{# Narrative blocks for the student guide. Every number comes from the context computed in
   scripts/build_student_guide.py from results/pilot, so the text cannot drift from the data.
   Blocks are guarded with `if` so a partially finished grid still renders honestly. #}

{% macro run_line(c, rid, name) -%}
{%- set r = c.runs[rid] -%}
<b>{{ name }}</b>: {{ "%.1f"|format(100 * c.base_asr[rid.split('-')[0]]) }}% → {{ r.asr }}
({{ "%+.1f"|format(r.delta_pp) }} points; {{ r.broken }} prompts newly broken, {{ r.fixed }} newly fixed,
p = {{ "%.2g"|format(r.p) }})
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro tldr_html(c) %}
<ul>
<li><b>The question.</b> Open models can be trained to ignore instructions hidden in the data they read
  (prompt-injection "hardening"). Companies then fine-tune those models on their own data. Does the hardening
  survive?</li>
<li><b>Hardening works.</b> A small replication of the SecAlign recipe cut the attack success rate (ASR) of
  Qwen2.5-0.5B from {{ c.evals['eval-qwen-undefended'].asr }} to {{ c.main.asr }}, and of SmolLM2-360M from
  {{ c.evals['eval-smol-undefended'].asr }} to {{ c.evals['eval-smol-secalign'].asr }} — but only when untrusted
  data is kept in its own <code>input</code> role, and at a cost in usefulness.</li>
{% if 'qwen-dolly-lora' in c.runs and 'smol-dolly-lora' in c.runs %}
<li><b>Ordinary fine-tuning quietly undoes part of it.</b> General instruction tuning on the benign Dolly dataset
  made both models significantly easier to hijack — {{ run_line(c, 'qwen-dolly-lora', 'Qwen') }};
  {{ run_line(c, 'smol-dolly-lora', 'SmolLM2') }} — while their measured usefulness went <em>up</em>
  ({{ c.runs['qwen-dolly-lora'].utility }} and {{ c.runs['smol-dolly-lora'].utility }} correct answers). A team
  watching only task metrics would ship the weaker model.</li>
{% endif %}
{{ c.extra_tldr | default('') | safe }}
<li><b>The margin erodes even when the headline number doesn't.</b> The probability the model gives the
  injected reply rose in {{ c.n_lp_up }} of {{ c.n_plain }} plain fine-tunes, including ones whose attack success rate
  did not change. In the Qwen Dolly run it had moved most of the way within the first third of training, while
  the attack success rate on the tracking set had barely moved — a signal a CI job can watch.</li>
<li><b>It can be caught automatically.</b> The project's CI gate, which compares a fine-tune with its hardened base
  on the same prompts, flags {{ c.gate_counts.fail }} of {{ c.gate_counts.total }} fine-tunes in the pilot.</li>
</ul>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_hardening(c) %}
<p>Read the table in pairs of rows. For Qwen2.5-0.5B, mini-SecAlign cut static ASR from
{{ c.evals['eval-qwen-undefended'].asr }} to {{ c.main.asr }} and the worst case over attack variants (best-of-K)
from {{ c.evals['eval-qwen-undefended'].bestk }} to {{ c.main.bestk }}. Three lessons:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>The defense needs its front-end.</b> The same hardened model is much easier to hijack when the data is
  pasted into the user turn ({{ c.evals_naive['eval-qwen-secalign'].asr }}) instead of the <code>input</code> role.</li>
<li><b>It costs usefulness.</b> Clean-question accuracy fell from {{ c.evals['eval-qwen-undefended'].utility }} to
  {{ c.main.utility }} (Qwen) and from {{ c.evals['eval-smol-undefended'].utility }} to
  {{ c.evals['eval-smol-secalign'].utility }} (SmolLM2). Meta SecAlign reports the same tension at scale.</li>
<li><b>More training is not always more security.</b> The 1,600-pair ablation is both less robust and less useful
  than the 400-pair model.</li>
</ul>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_erosion(c) %}
<p>Each row in the tables below is one ordinary fine-tune of a hardened model, evaluated on exactly the same 1,216
prompts as its base. "Broken / fixed" counts the prompts that flipped, which is what the McNemar p-value is computed
from. Of the {{ c.n_plain }} plain fine-tunes in the pilot:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>{{ c.sig_runs | length }} made the model significantly easier to hijack</b> (p &lt; 0.05):
  {{ c.sig_runs | join('; ') }}.{% if c.n_util_up_sig %} In {{ c.n_util_up_sig }} of them the model's clean-task
  accuracy went <em>up</em> at the same time — exactly the situation in which nobody would think to re-test
  security.{% endif %}</li>
<li><b>{{ c.nonsig_runs | length }} did not</b>: {{ c.nonsig_runs | join('; ') }}.{% if c.nonsig_all_no_channel and c.sig_all_channel %}
  Notice the split: every recipe that eroded robustness trains on examples where retrieved context, tool output or
  other data sits in the user turn next to an instruction, and every recipe that did not has no such data channel.
  The fine-tune is literally teaching the model "content in the user turn is something to act on".{% endif %}</li>
<li><b>Inside the model the margin shrank in {{ c.n_lp_up }} of {{ c.n_plain }} runs</b>: the log-probability of the
  injected reply rose even when the attack success rate did not.</li>
</ul>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_tracking(c) %}
{% if 'qwen-dolly-lora' in c.runs %}
{% set r = c.runs['qwen-dolly-lora'] %}
<p>The tracker measured each model at ⅓ and ⅔ of fine-tuning. Take the Dolly run on Qwen: at the ⅓ mark the attack
success rate on the tracking set was still {{ "%.1f"|format(100 * r.track_asr[1]) }}% (the base was
{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * r.track_asr[0]) }}%), yet the log-probability the model assigns to the injected reply had
already jumped from {{ "%.1f"|format(r.lp0) }} toward {{ "%.1f"|format(r.lp1) }} — the injected answer became
roughly {{ "%.0f"|format((2.718281828 ** (r.lp1 - r.lp0))) }}× more likely per token by the end — and the frozen
role-probe separation d′ had dropped from {{ "%.1f"|format(r.d0) }} to {{ "%.1f"|format(r.d1) }}. The visible attack
success rate followed later. This is what "quiet" erosion looks like: the safety <em>margin</em> shrinks first.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_probes(c) %}
{% if c.probe_corr %}
<p>Across all runs, how well does the change in each internal signal track the change in attack success? A
correlation near +1 means "when this signal goes up, robustness goes down"; near −1 the opposite; near 0, no
relation. With a dozen runs these are rough numbers, but they say which probes are worth watching in a CI job.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_gcg(c) %}
{{ c.gcg_text | default('<p>GCG results are added when the adaptive-attack runs finish.</p>') | safe }}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro mit_line(c, rid, name) -%}
{%- if rid in c.runs -%}{%- set r = c.runs[rid] -%}
<li><b>{{ name }}</b>: ASR {{ r.asr }} ({{ "%+.1f"|format(r.delta_pp) }} points vs the hardened base; {{ r.broken }}
  prompts newly broken, {{ r.fixed }} newly fixed), best-of-K {{ r.bestk }}, utility {{ r.utility }}, held-out Dolly
  loss {{ "%.3f"|format(r.task_after) }}.</li>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro r_mitigation(c) %}
{% if 'qwen-dolly-lora' in c.runs and 'qwen-dolly-lora-secanchor' in c.runs %}
<p>All methods below fine-tune the hardened Qwen on the same 768 Dolly examples for the same number of steps. The
plain run is the one that eroded robustness in chapter 6.</p>
<ul>
{{ mit_line(c, 'qwen-dolly-lora', 'Plain fine-tune') }}
{{ mit_line(c, 'qwen-dolly-lora-replay', 'Replay') }}
{{ mit_line(c, 'qwen-dolly-lora-reharden', 'Re-harden afterwards') }}
{{ mit_line(c, 'qwen-dolly-lora-secanchor', 'SecAnchor') }}
</ul>
{% set p = c.runs['qwen-dolly-lora'] %}{% set s = c.runs['qwen-dolly-lora-secanchor'] %}
<p>How to read this: the held-out Dolly loss tells you whether the model still learned its new job — SecAnchor's
{{ "%.3f"|format(s.task_after) }} versus {{ "%.3f"|format(p.task_after) }} for the plain run means it did. At the same
time its attack success rate ended <em>below</em> the hardened base it started from, because the anchor term keeps
SecAlign's objective alive during the fine-tune. The price is compute (the anchor pairs cost about as much as the
task batch for short examples like Dolly's) and a smaller utility gain than the plain fine-tune.</p>
{% if 'qwen-dolly-lora-replay' in c.runs %}{% set rp = c.runs['qwen-dolly-lora-replay'] %}
<p><b>Why not just mix in some security examples?</b> That is what <em>replay</em> does: one hardening-style example (an
injected prompt with the correct answer) in every training step. It ended at {{ rp.asr }} —
{% if rp.asr_raw > p.asr_raw %}<em>worse</em> than the plain fine-tune{% else %}no better than the plain fine-tune{% endif %}.
Supervised replay only makes the right answer more likely; it never makes the injected answer <em>less</em> likely.
SecAnchor's preference term does both, and that difference is the whole result.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if 'qwen-rag-lora' in c.runs and 'qwen-rag-secure-lora' in c.runs %}
{% set a = c.runs['qwen-rag-lora'] %}{% set b = c.runs['qwen-rag-secure-lora'] %}
<p><b>The free option: format the fine-tuning data securely.</b> Fine-tuning on RAG data with the retrieved context in the
user turn gave ASR {{ a.asr }} ({{ "%+.1f"|format(a.delta_pp) }} points); the same data with the context in the
<code>input</code> role gave {{ b.asr }} ({{ "%+.1f"|format(b.delta_pp) }} points).</p>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro r_gate(c) %}
<p>Replaying the gate's regression rule on the stored results (absolute ceilings switched off, since even the
hardened base is not at 0%): a fine-tune fails if its ASR rose by more than 2 points <em>and</em> the paired test is
significant. It flags {{ c.gate_counts.fail }} of the {{ c.gate_counts.total }} pilot fine-tunes:</p>
{% endmacro %}
